
1. Palindrome Number
====================
Reverse the digits mathematically using modulo (% 10) and division (/ 10).
java
public static boolean isPalindrome(int n) {
    if (n < 0) return false;
    int original = n, reversed = 0;
    while (n > 0) {
        int digit = n % 10;
        reversed = reversed * 10 + digit;
        n /= 10;
    }
    return original == reversed;
}
Use code with caution.
• Time Complexity: \(O(\log_{10} n)\) (runs once per digit).
• Space Complexity: O(1).

2. Armstrong Number
===================
Count the digits first, then sum each digit raised to that power.
java
public static boolean isArmstrong(int n) {
    if (n < 0) return false;
    int original = n, sum = 0;
    int digits = String.valueOf(n).length();
    
    while (n > 0) {
        int digit = n % 10;
        sum += Math.pow(digit, digits);
        n /= 10;
    }
    return original == sum;
}
Use code with caution.
• Time Complexity: \(O(\log_{10} n)\).
• Space Complexity: O(1).

3. Prime Number
==================
Only check divisors up to \(\sqrt{n}\). Any factor larger than \(\sqrt{n}\) pairs with a factor smaller than \(\sqrt{n}\).
java
public static boolean isPrime(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return false;
    if (n == 2 || n == 3) return true;
    if (n % 2 == 0 || n % 3 == 0) return false;
    
    for (int i = 5; i * i <= n; i += 6) {
        if (n % i == 0 || n % (i + 2) == 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
}
Use code with caution.
• Time Complexity: \(O(\sqrt{n})\).
• Space Complexity: O(1).

4. Fibonacci Series
====================
Use an iterative approach with two variables instead of recursion to avoid exponential slowdowns and stack overflow.
java
public static void printFibonacci(int count) {
    int a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        System.out.print(a + " ");
        int next = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = next;
    }
}
Use code with caution.
• Time Complexity: O(n).
• Space Complexity: O(1).

5. Perfect Number
=================
Loop up to \(\sqrt{n}\) to find proper divisors in pairs, adding both i and n / i.
java
public static boolean isPerfect(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return false;
    int sum = 1; // 1 is always a proper divisor
    
    for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            sum += i;
            if (i != n / i) {
                sum += n / i;
            }
        }
    }
    return sum == n;
}
Use code with caution.
• Time Complexity: \(O(\sqrt{n})\).
• Space Complexity: O(1).

6. Strong Number
===============
Precompute factorials for digits 0 through 9 in an array so you don't recalculate them inside the loop.
java
public static boolean isStrong(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false;
    int[] fact = {1, 1, 2, 6, 24, 120, 720, 5040, 40320, 362880};
    int original = n, sum = 0;
    
    while (n > 0) {
        sum += fact[n % 10];
        n /= 10;
    }
    return original == sum;
}
Use code with caution.
• Time Complexity: \(O(\log_{10} n)\).
• Space Complexity: O(1).

Quick Interview Tips

• Prevent Integer Overflow: When reversing digits or summing cubes/factorials, interviewers often ask what happens if n is near Integer.MAX_VALUE. Mention using long for reversed or sum to prevent overflow.
• Avoid Math.pow() if asked: In strict interviews, interviewers may ask you to write a simple integer power helper loop instead of Math.pow(), since Math.pow() returns a double and can have floating-point precision quirks on very large number